 {:db/id "q-20849" :question/id #uuid "45b1f64f-9248-57a2-a310-050eb4c80ee5" :question/math-academy-id "q-20849" :question/problem "![](images/c9/c9371d5e2bb921b4f5e58423bbce492f2b555122ee42bd861040b57d01070353.png)\n\nFor the parametric curve (shown above)\n\n$$\nx=2(t+1),\\,y=4{t}^{2},\\,-1\\le t<1,\n$$\n\n find the derivative $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ at the point $P$ on the curve where $t=0.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=2,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=8t.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{8t}{2}=4t.\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=0$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =4(0) \\\\  & =0\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-20849/ma-source-field/e15aa3980e973581b3107ffc08abdc430abb0b5426cd68af5d4ac003fc981600"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
