 {:db/id "q-21496/ma-source-field/e44d1ec315c16231fdb8d59e7d5fd9404991b33f85651a06f9c6b57c4e523650" :answer-field/id #uuid "5e6ea143-756a-5eb6-a6ed-9e3449dbdb6b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/choices ["q-21496/ma-source-field/e44d1ec315c16231fdb8d59e7d5fd9404991b33f85651a06f9c6b57c4e523650/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" "q-21496/ma-source-field/e44d1ec315c16231fdb8d59e7d5fd9404991b33f85651a06f9c6b57c4e523650/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" "q-21496/ma-source-field/e44d1ec315c16231fdb8d59e7d5fd9404991b33f85651a06f9c6b57c4e523650/af5580a233ee3709064249d6732fc822dfb93f38caf90c025deb51b127e90b2c" "q-21496/ma-source-field/e44d1ec315c16231fdb8d59e7d5fd9404991b33f85651a06f9c6b57c4e523650/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" "q-21496/ma-source-field/e44d1ec315c16231fdb8d59e7d5fd9404991b33f85651a06f9c6b57c4e523650/2edf353dc47d221855e5e7ee4be942392390ff01766e089c29dc368c2051b12d"] :answer-field/correct "q-21496/ma-source-field/e44d1ec315c16231fdb8d59e7d5fd9404991b33f85651a06f9c6b57c4e523650/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]}
 {:db/id "q-21496" :question/id #uuid "6bb098c4-0b9d-56fe-8eb4-70ba83a986f8" :question/math-academy-id "q-21496" :question/problem "For which value of $x$ does the function $f(x)={x}^{3}+\\frac{48}{x}$ defined for $x\\in (0,\\infty )$ have a local minimum?" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =3{x}^{2}-\\frac{48}{{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ on $(0,\\infty )$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-\\frac{48}{{x}^{2}} & =0 \\\\ 3{x}^{4}-48 & =0 \\\\ 3({x}^{4}-16) & =0 \\\\ 3({x}^{2}+4)({x}^{2}-4) & =0 \\\\ 3({x}^{2}+4)(x+2)(x-2) & =0 \\\\ x & =\\pm 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $x=2$ is a stationary point on $(0,\\infty ).$ Next, to determine whether this point is a local maximum or minimum, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=1,3$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(1) & =3(1)^{2}-\\frac{48}{{1}^{2}}=-45<0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(3) & =3(3)^{2}-\\frac{48}{{3}^{2}}=27-\\frac{16}{3}=\\frac{65}{3}>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(0,2)$ | $2$ | $(2,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $1$ |  | $3$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n| $f$ | $↘$ | min | $↗$ |\n\nFinally, let's use the table above to classify the critical point.\n\n- At $x=2,$ the first derivative changes sign from negative to positive. Therefore, $x=2$ is a local minimum.\n\nWe conclude that $f(x)$ has a local minimum at $x=2.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-21496/ma-source-field/e44d1ec315c16231fdb8d59e7d5fd9404991b33f85651a06f9c6b57c4e523650"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
