 {:db/id "q-2549" :question/id #uuid "236c5a80-1a72-598b-b891-11878898b571" :question/math-academy-id "q-2549" :question/problem "![](images/27/2771f90d4200f292afe8108a26b8a2e83e6f3a46194c89a18d1688943984b5af.png)\n\nA spherical balloon is inflated so that its radius increases at a rate of $1\\,\\text{cm/s}.$ How fast is the volume of the balloon increasing at the moment when the radius is $2\\,\\text{cm}?$" :question/worked-solution "Let $r$ be the radius of the balloon and $V$ its volume. Both $r$ and $V$ change with time. In particular, we are given\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=1\\,\\text{cm/s},\n$$\n\nand we want to find $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}$ when $r=2\\,\\text{cm}.$\n\nTo start, let's write down the volume formula for the sphere and then differentiate it using implicit differentiation. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {r}^{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(V) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {r}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}r}(\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {r}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 4\\pi {r}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we can substitute the given information $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=1$ and $r=2,$ and solve for $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =1\\cdot 4\\pi (2)^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =16\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of the balloon is increasing at a rate of $16\\pi {\\text{ cm}}^{3}\\text{/s}$\n when the radius is $2\\,\\text{cm}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-2549/ma-source-field/dca90a9c3e5a3a8340a6bd9c76376af96866513c569f446ffb615a31cb69e3f1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
