 {:db/id "q-32531/ma-source-field/cff88df0a0e62b0ae4132dd30db0d5a707cf7586cff5ce9ddf0c53ef7918958d" :answer-field/id #uuid "9431c4a2-40f0-53be-95c1-e0f65f36ff4d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/choices ["q-32531/ma-source-field/cff88df0a0e62b0ae4132dd30db0d5a707cf7586cff5ce9ddf0c53ef7918958d/4e2cb386499e1926f074a7bcd422a63175a237204a3823f0fa6c86b56d301873" "q-32531/ma-source-field/cff88df0a0e62b0ae4132dd30db0d5a707cf7586cff5ce9ddf0c53ef7918958d/2aef90071200a7bc65baa2d03f49803233ca7b2dc16865265136281ee914878b" "q-32531/ma-source-field/cff88df0a0e62b0ae4132dd30db0d5a707cf7586cff5ce9ddf0c53ef7918958d/681a4b64a7bb464b7f554c4e26477e3547d3c60cd353fce5dda4ae108b1c4fa5" "q-32531/ma-source-field/cff88df0a0e62b0ae4132dd30db0d5a707cf7586cff5ce9ddf0c53ef7918958d/3089f9a34f7a805d8b2b2d5d51b10dc7aea027e8ec8fd8d06ad11cecf2ba9740" "q-32531/ma-source-field/cff88df0a0e62b0ae4132dd30db0d5a707cf7586cff5ce9ddf0c53ef7918958d/4280bcd0914e40dbdc039bc97ee5d5baafd2654e75e9b692316d9c28c4842856"] :answer-field/correct "q-32531/ma-source-field/cff88df0a0e62b0ae4132dd30db0d5a707cf7586cff5ce9ddf0c53ef7918958d/4280bcd0914e40dbdc039bc97ee5d5baafd2654e75e9b692316d9c28c4842856" :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]}
 {:db/id "q-32531" :question/id #uuid "98334c69-2d0e-5223-82ea-d968a6c359cb" :question/math-academy-id "q-32531" :question/problem "Given that \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 5 & 10\\end{aligned}]$ has the LU decomposition $A=LU$, then ${l}_{21}+{u}_{22}=$" :question/worked-solution "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 5 & 10\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{5}{2}){R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{5}{2} & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{5}{2} & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{5}{2} & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}{{[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{21}=\\frac{5}{2},{u}_{22}=0,$ and ${l}_{21}+{u}_{22}=\\frac{5}{2}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-32531/ma-source-field/cff88df0a0e62b0ae4132dd30db0d5a707cf7586cff5ce9ddf0c53ef7918958d"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
