 {:db/id "q-34422/ma-source-field/2afb61033b808f6f20c3e938a4c902e2617107dcb73dc02e3db8ed5b0c9b418f" :answer-field/id #uuid "eb2dd0d4-9bed-58b4-940b-d9ecf1e1866c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/choices ["q-34422/ma-source-field/2afb61033b808f6f20c3e938a4c902e2617107dcb73dc02e3db8ed5b0c9b418f/eddc584b31d4a80179e9498da1aeab49ea2c84f0426c04a753bb5632fe5ce44f" "q-34422/ma-source-field/2afb61033b808f6f20c3e938a4c902e2617107dcb73dc02e3db8ed5b0c9b418f/28b4bae639790da58210f04cd074f081ca0602cd8cac01fc317c1b731615c933" "q-34422/ma-source-field/2afb61033b808f6f20c3e938a4c902e2617107dcb73dc02e3db8ed5b0c9b418f/e5402976e3454c3d42a7c892015d52b6504db08e26b9754c64ebeccd6f821bf7" "q-34422/ma-source-field/2afb61033b808f6f20c3e938a4c902e2617107dcb73dc02e3db8ed5b0c9b418f/df8719ac67769c06bc5471cbdd033e807b93a828dc1b6cc6509a271d1fca798f" "q-34422/ma-source-field/2afb61033b808f6f20c3e938a4c902e2617107dcb73dc02e3db8ed5b0c9b418f/f639f150287a95bee3e517379c75de510a4cd9e3bc359eed9d03a9e594f824fc"] :answer-field/correct "q-34422/ma-source-field/2afb61033b808f6f20c3e938a4c902e2617107dcb73dc02e3db8ed5b0c9b418f/eddc584b31d4a80179e9498da1aeab49ea2c84f0426c04a753bb5632fe5ce44f" :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]}
 {:db/id "q-34422" :question/id #uuid "4b9db0d1-0644-55bd-9fa5-174e2a1643f2" :question/math-academy-id "q-34422" :question/problem "Given the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 9 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 6 & 3\\end{aligned}]$, find a basis of $\\text{Null}(A).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we reduce the matrix $A$ to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 9 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 6 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-4){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 6 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation $Ax=0$ is equivalent to the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution is ${x}_{1}=-{x}_{3}$ and ${x}_{2}=0,$ where ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. Therefore, if $x\\in \\text{Null}(A),$ then\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{3} \\\\ 0 \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]={x}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nThis implies that \n\n$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}\n$$\n\nis a basis of $\\text{Null}(A)$ since it is a linearly independent set and its vectors generate $\\text{Null}(A).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-34422/ma-source-field/2afb61033b808f6f20c3e938a4c902e2617107dcb73dc02e3db8ed5b0c9b418f"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
