 {:db/id "q-36751/ma-source-field/2bf811cd2c5d4c53b2d24d2d049063dba31f8e17898697f9ea528244c26b5f2e" :answer-field/id #uuid "307532aa-e20c-56a5-8f44-7fdbefdbc75e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/choices ["q-36751/ma-source-field/2bf811cd2c5d4c53b2d24d2d049063dba31f8e17898697f9ea528244c26b5f2e/a3a5487afcc81a1c06c56ceaee82744a099a79debc8be0e8469f77e68bf12a04" "q-36751/ma-source-field/2bf811cd2c5d4c53b2d24d2d049063dba31f8e17898697f9ea528244c26b5f2e/f98c18a27dcb118caf5a5576c91aab2f7c6ef2dcdc9bdea8485ada011f865eac" "q-36751/ma-source-field/2bf811cd2c5d4c53b2d24d2d049063dba31f8e17898697f9ea528244c26b5f2e/82c79a11c5f316588a4977bf9de059017422d38604e630b6407357df3b622d75" "q-36751/ma-source-field/2bf811cd2c5d4c53b2d24d2d049063dba31f8e17898697f9ea528244c26b5f2e/0e3bdff03e951f0a388d8b8654d11436ca9d62823f0936e95aee0fe9e94cf8ad" "q-36751/ma-source-field/2bf811cd2c5d4c53b2d24d2d049063dba31f8e17898697f9ea528244c26b5f2e/d23151519d79f27e4cebc4ebad9047e36b8453244e6e3d202a56b90321183388"] :answer-field/correct "q-36751/ma-source-field/2bf811cd2c5d4c53b2d24d2d049063dba31f8e17898697f9ea528244c26b5f2e/82c79a11c5f316588a4977bf9de059017422d38604e630b6407357df3b622d75" :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]}
 {:db/id "q-36751" :question/id #uuid "d8eadfe0-8324-566b-8c56-2906c1bb6d35" :question/math-academy-id "q-36751" :question/problem "Find an eigenvector of\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =3.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =3.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]-3[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors corresponding to this eigenvalue, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -2{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+\\frac{1}{4}{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns (the $2$nd and the $3$rd). Thus, ${x}_{1}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain ${x}_{3}=0.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we get ${x}_{2}=0.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{1}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{1}=1,$ we get the eigenvector\n$v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-36751/ma-source-field/2bf811cd2c5d4c53b2d24d2d049063dba31f8e17898697f9ea528244c26b5f2e"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
