 {:db/id "q-36911/ma-source-field/a4e2d59120f3312b1505a2fed52b1eed7f5f463de87650d9a9f00d433a06b886" :answer-field/id #uuid "3ad0dc77-76f9-5eaa-aa66-ed1f16aa44f6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/choices ["q-36911/ma-source-field/a4e2d59120f3312b1505a2fed52b1eed7f5f463de87650d9a9f00d433a06b886/228d7b42fcb34515143bb5c9b68cc81052255c360fff1053199a2147dbd7eb12" "q-36911/ma-source-field/a4e2d59120f3312b1505a2fed52b1eed7f5f463de87650d9a9f00d433a06b886/aca3fb0d9ddb49350c290ac929c3cc1d4fe3619668acb3d7b8d6d89671359f83" "q-36911/ma-source-field/a4e2d59120f3312b1505a2fed52b1eed7f5f463de87650d9a9f00d433a06b886/7a5aab969ffcdbe5ac6bd1a68897506c93bac105dd752828cf140c8ec9e1f647" "q-36911/ma-source-field/a4e2d59120f3312b1505a2fed52b1eed7f5f463de87650d9a9f00d433a06b886/981a2951e3fccc9ba81c501537ab818060069591c658c07fba6936fa5fd0dd27" "q-36911/ma-source-field/a4e2d59120f3312b1505a2fed52b1eed7f5f463de87650d9a9f00d433a06b886/464b9ac5ac22bfcc69adaf42253a8d7a5eef4a84e7c96c7715039d251f95788a"] :answer-field/correct "q-36911/ma-source-field/a4e2d59120f3312b1505a2fed52b1eed7f5f463de87650d9a9f00d433a06b886/aca3fb0d9ddb49350c290ac929c3cc1d4fe3619668acb3d7b8d6d89671359f83" :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]}
 {:db/id "q-36911" :question/id #uuid "7795ea1b-86ec-5a3d-baba-dbdd67574697" :question/math-academy-id "q-36911" :question/problem "Given that $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)+2(y-3)^{2}$, find the points $(x,y)$ where $∇f(x,y)=0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)+2(y-3)^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}\\cdot 2x+0 \\\\  & =\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+1} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)+2(y-3)^{2}) \\\\  & =0+2\\cdot 2(y-3) \\\\  & =4(y-3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇f(x,y)=\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,i+4(y-3)\\,j.$\n\nNow, we solve $∇f(x,y)=0:$ \n\n$$\n\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,i+4(y-3)\\,j=0\\text{ }i+0\\text{ }j\n$$\n\nSo, by equating the coefficients of $i$ and $j,$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+1}=0 \\\\ 4(y-3)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we get $x=0$ and from the second equation, we obtain $y=3.$ \n\nTherefore, $∇f(x,y)=0$ at the point $(0,3).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-36911/ma-source-field/a4e2d59120f3312b1505a2fed52b1eed7f5f463de87650d9a9f00d433a06b886"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
