 {:db/id "q-37846/ma-source-field/d809a074c41dc0409abec96d379bea2339297729d60ea9f5e3f5e66a75391fb1" :answer-field/id #uuid "72ff45fc-3165-5e0a-8ffa-750ac771413e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/choices ["q-37846/ma-source-field/d809a074c41dc0409abec96d379bea2339297729d60ea9f5e3f5e66a75391fb1/3c18e8dc6829c868136a882a333831910c94a82cbcf98128fd48c94767f8da39" "q-37846/ma-source-field/d809a074c41dc0409abec96d379bea2339297729d60ea9f5e3f5e66a75391fb1/c876e58aef36217bdae1fbcbb1f0642503a63d46390e7a6e0752f271b6fa5fb7" "q-37846/ma-source-field/d809a074c41dc0409abec96d379bea2339297729d60ea9f5e3f5e66a75391fb1/9ab2e50ba0ae37560fb1b9de01e383867a251e895196b122d2c3ba9f10667d7d" "q-37846/ma-source-field/d809a074c41dc0409abec96d379bea2339297729d60ea9f5e3f5e66a75391fb1/38afe3565f8d0326dd8d3c9f4616ed1d358bce4b7314bc02aee4958e15c65903" "q-37846/ma-source-field/d809a074c41dc0409abec96d379bea2339297729d60ea9f5e3f5e66a75391fb1/fde7c7e8c5c0e1280c4619c1897573fc8396a25055092e595f0052889d677717"] :answer-field/correct "q-37846/ma-source-field/d809a074c41dc0409abec96d379bea2339297729d60ea9f5e3f5e66a75391fb1/3c18e8dc6829c868136a882a333831910c94a82cbcf98128fd48c94767f8da39" :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]}
 {:db/id "q-37846" :question/id #uuid "21c29510-ffed-5744-82ad-55435a6f5d16" :question/math-academy-id "q-37846" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A,$ given by\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -9 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 4 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nDetermine whether $A=PD{P}^{-1}$ for some invertible matrix $P$ and a diagonal matrix $D,$ where $P$ and $D$ are both real. In the affirmative case, find a possible diagonal matrix $D.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to determine whether $D$ is diagonalizable over $R.$ \n\nWe start by computing the eigenvalues of $A.$ To do that, we solve the characteristic equation, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -9-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 4 & 4 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-9-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & 4 \\\\ 4 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-9-\\lambda )((1-\\lambda )(1-\\lambda )-16) & =0 \\\\ (-9-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda -15) & =0 \\\\ (-9-\\lambda )(\\lambda +3)(\\lambda -5) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues of $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-9,\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=-3$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=5.$ Note that all $\\lambda $'s are simple roots of the characteristic polynomial.\n\nSince all of the eigenvalues are simple roots of the characteristic polynomial, $A$ is diagonalizable. \n\nWe construct a diagonal matrix $D$ by writing the eigenvalues in the main diagonal. Therefore, one possible matrix $D$ is\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}-9 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\,\\,-3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNote that the diagonal matrix $D$ is unique up to the ordering of the eigenvalues. Therefore, another possible diagonal matrix is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\,\\,-9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-37846/ma-source-field/d809a074c41dc0409abec96d379bea2339297729d60ea9f5e3f5e66a75391fb1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
