 {:db/id [:question/id #uuid "326587d3-f0fa-5430-a120-481fbe187c4a"] :question/problem "Let\n$T=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 6\\end{aligned}]$\nbe the standard matrix of the linear transformation $T.$ Then $T$ maps ${R}^{2}$ onto" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $T$ has proportional rows, and yet $T$ is not the zero matrix. \n\nTherefore, $\\text{rank}(T)=1$ and $T$ maps ${R}^{2}$ onto a line that passes through the origin.\n\nMoreover, the line must pass through the origin $(0,0)$ and the points $(1,3)$ and $(2,6),$ which are defined by the columns of the matrix $T.$ \n\nComputing the slope $m$ of this line, we get\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{6-3}{2-1}=3.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, $T$ maps ${R}^{2}$ onto the line $y=3x.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-38229/ma-source-field/8a232d69219d73f74b4c197045e9521474a3e0e1f1466b5437b802d8bbbcfcf5"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
