 {:db/id "q-6664" :question/id #uuid "b33211c6-2184-5991-90d3-02829e541c3c" :question/math-academy-id "q-6664" :question/problem "According to the rational roots theorem, which of the following could be a root of the polynomial $f(x)=2{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}-8x-3?$" :question/worked-solution "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $-3$ and the leading coefficient is $2.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }-3}{\\text{factor of }2}.\n$$\n\nOf the given answer choices, only $-\\frac{3}{2}$ takes the above form since the numerator $(3)$ is a factor of $-3$ and the denominator $(2)$ is a factor of $2.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-6664/ma-source-field/8d930d297ed5be0282578fe372ebde2197efa929799a7be205c76b2134c7e5d8"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
