 {:db/id [:question/id #uuid "edc161f0-8951-58a5-87a8-35a7ed39116e"] :question/problem "According to the rational roots theorem, which of the following **cannot** be a root of the polynomial $f(x)=4{x}^{3}-6{x}^{2}-10x-4?$" :question/worked-solution "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $-4$ and the leading coefficient is $4.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }-4}{\\text{factor of }4}.\n$$\n\nOf the given answer choices, only $-6$ does not take the above form. The value $-6$ can be interpreted as a fraction $-\\frac{6}{1},$ and the numerator $(6)$ is not a factor of $-4.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-6671/ma-source-field/968caa56e409c39e35cc6396b2590279c934e1ab600786ae8e2e8b047908f3a1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
