 {:db/id "q-9455" :question/id #uuid "ede784c5-2ada-5719-b4c5-5216ffbee9f0" :question/math-academy-id "q-9455" :question/problem "Calculate the point where the ellipse $\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{(y-3)^{2}}{9}=1$ intercepts the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "To calculate the $x$-intercept, we need to substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the ellipse and solve the resulting equation for $x.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{(y-3)^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{(0-3)^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{9}{9} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{3}+1 & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{3} & =0 \\\\ (x-1)^{2} & =0 \\\\ x-1 & =0 \\\\ x & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse intercepts the $x$-axis at $(1,0).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-9455/ma-source-field/e1df2a8454b5db1c426e40647ede9e07ae262d0a6898670ae0af63256b29c06e"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
